::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which tool is used to transfer the length of a segment during a construction? {
~A. Protractor#A protractor measures angles, not segment lengths.
=B. Compass#Correct. A compass holds a fixed opening to transfer a segment length.
~C. Calculator#A calculator does not transfer geometric distances.
~D. Notebook margin#A margin is not a construction tool.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>If segment CD is constructed as a copy of segment AB, which statement should be true? {
~A. CD > AB#A copied segment should not be longer than the original.
~B. CD < AB#A copied segment should not be shorter than the original.
~C. C = D#Points are locations, not segment lengths.
=D. CD = AB#Correct. A copied segment has the same length as the original.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>When copying &angle;ABC, where is the first compass arc centered? {
=A. At B#Correct. B is the vertex of &angle;ABC.
~B. At A#A is on a side of the angle, not the vertex.
~C. At C#C is on a side of the angle, not the vertex.
~D. At any point by sight#The arc must be centered at the angle vertex.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which action would make a copied segment unreliable? {
~A. Drawing a ray from the new starting point#A starting ray is part of the construction.
~B. Placing the compass point on the new endpoint#This is needed to transfer the length.
=C. Changing the compass opening before marking the endpoint#Correct. Changing the opening changes the transferred length.
~D. Marking the arc intersection on the ray#This marks the copied segment endpoint.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>If &angle;DEF is a copy of &angle;ABC, which statement should be true? {
=A. m&angle;DEF = m&angle;ABC#Correct. A copied angle has the same measure as the original.
~B. m&angle;DEF > m&angle;ABC#A copied angle should not be larger than the original.
~C. m&angle;DEF < m&angle;ABC#A copied angle should not be smaller than the original.
~D. DE = AB#Copying an angle does not require these segment lengths to match.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Why is estimating by sight not enough when copying an angle? {
~A. Sight estimation always creates equal angle measures#Sight estimation does not guarantee equality.
~B. Angle copies never require tools#This standard requires geometric tools.
~C. A copied angle must be larger than the original#A copy should match the original angle measure.
=D. Appearance alone does not prove the angle measure was preserved#Correct. Arcs and compass distances provide construction evidence.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>What does a segment bisector do? {
~A. Copies a segment to a new ray#That describes segment-copy construction.
=B. Divides a segment into two equal parts#Correct. A segment bisector creates two congruent segment parts.
~C. Measures an angle in degrees#A segment bisector works with segment length.
~D. Renames the endpoints#Naming endpoints does not bisect a segment.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>If M is the midpoint of segment AB, which statement is true? {
~A. AM > MB#A midpoint creates equal parts.
~B. AB = MB#The whole segment is not equal to one part.
=C. AM = MB#Correct. A midpoint divides a segment into two equal lengths.
~D. A = B#Distinct endpoints are not equal segment lengths.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>What must be true about a perpendicular bisector of AB? {
~A. It only crosses endpoint A#A perpendicular bisector crosses the segment at its midpoint.
~B. It copies AB onto a new ray#That is a segment-copy construction.
~C. It changes the length of AB#The original segment length does not change.
=D. It crosses AB at its midpoint and forms right angles#Correct. Both midpoint and perpendicular relationships are required.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>If ray BD bisects &angle;ABC, which statement is true? {
=A. m&angle;ABD = m&angle;DBC#Correct. The ray divides the angle into two congruent angles.
~B. m&angle;ABD > m&angle;DBC#An angle bisector creates equal angle measures.
~C. AB = BC#Angle bisection does not require equal side lengths.
~D. BD = AB#Ray length is not the angle-bisector conclusion.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q11::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Why are matching compass openings important when constructing a bisector? {
~A. They make the drawing darker#The purpose is accuracy, not appearance.
~B. They remove the need for a straightedge#A straightedge is still used to draw the final line or ray.
=C. They provide evidence of equal distances#Correct. Equal compass openings support equal-distance relationships.
~D. They measure every angle in degrees#A compass transfers distance, not degree measure.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q12::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which evidence best supports that line &ell; is the perpendicular bisector of AB at M? {
~A. Line &ell; looks centered on AB#Appearance alone is not enough evidence.
=B. AM = MB and line &ell; forms right angles with AB#Correct. A perpendicular bisector requires both conditions.
~C. Line &ell; is drawn longer than AB#Line length does not prove perpendicular bisection.
~D. A and B are labeled#Labels do not prove the required relationships.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q13::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>In If p, then q, what is p called? {
=A. The hypothesis#Correct. The hypothesis is the condition that follows if.
~B. The conclusion#The conclusion is q.
~C. The converse#The converse is If q, then p.
~D. The contrapositive#The contrapositive is If not q, then not p.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q14::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>What is the converse of If p, then q? {
~A. If not p, then not q#This is the inverse.
=B. If q, then p#Correct. The converse switches p and q.
~C. If not q, then not p#This is the contrapositive.
~D. If p, then not q#This is not the converse.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q15::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>What is the inverse of If p, then q? {
~A. If q, then p#This is the converse.
~B. If not q, then not p#This is the contrapositive.
~C. If p, then q#This is the original conditional.
=D. If not p, then not q#Correct. The inverse keeps the order and negates both parts.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q16::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>What is the contrapositive of If p, then q? {
~A. If q, then p#This is the converse.
~B. If not p, then not q#This is the inverse.
=C. If not q, then not p#Correct. The contrapositive switches and negates both parts.
~D. If p, then not q#This only negates q.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q17::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>What does if and only if mean? {
~A. Only the conditional direction is included#A biconditional includes both directions.
=B. Both the conditional and converse directions are included#Correct. If and only if means both directions hold.
~C. Only the inverse direction is included#The inverse is not the same as a biconditional.
~D. Both statement parts are negated#Negation does not create a biconditional.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q18::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which if-then statement matches All rectangles have four right angles? {
=A. If a figure is a rectangle, then it has four right angles#Correct. All rectangles means every rectangle has the property.
~B. If a figure has four right angles, then it is a rectangle#This is the converse.
~C. If a figure is not a rectangle, then it does not have four right angles#This is the inverse.
~D. If a figure does not have four right angles, then it is not a rectangle#This is the contrapositive.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q19::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>For If a figure is a square, then it is a rectangle, which statement is the contrapositive? {
~A. If a figure is a rectangle, then it is a square#This is the converse.
~B. If a figure is not a square, then it is not a rectangle#This is the inverse.
~C. If a figure is a square, then it is not a rectangle#This negates only the conclusion.
=D. If a figure is not a rectangle, then it is not a square#Correct. The contrapositive switches and negates both parts.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q20::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>What does not do in a related conditional statement? {
~A. It switches the order of p and q#Switching order creates a converse.
~B. It creates an if and only if statement#A biconditional is different from negation.
=C. It negates a statement part#Correct. Not denies the condition or conclusion.
~D. It removes the conclusion#The conclusion can still exist in negated form.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q21::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.1<br><br>Question:<br>If &angle;1 and &angle;2 are vertical angles, which statement is supported? {
~A. m&angle;1 + m&angle;2 = 180&deg;#Vertical angles are congruent, not always supplementary.
~B. &angle;1 and &angle;2 form a linear pair#Vertical angles are not adjacent linear-pair angles.
=C. &angle;1 &cong; &angle;2#Correct. Vertical angles are congruent.
~D. m&angle;1 = 90&deg;#Vertical angles are not always right angles.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q22::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.1<br><br>Question:<br>If &angle;A and &angle;B form a linear pair, which statement is true? {
~A. m&angle;A = m&angle;B#Linear-pair angles are not always congruent.
~B. m&angle;A + m&angle;B = 90&deg;#This is a right-angle sum, not a linear-pair sum.
~C. &angle;A and &angle;B are vertical angles#A linear pair is adjacent.
=D. m&angle;A + m&angle;B = 180&deg;#Correct. Linear-pair angles are supplementary.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q23::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.1<br><br>Question:<br>If two angles are supplementary, what is true about their measures? {
=A. They add to 180&deg;#Correct. Supplementary angles have measures that add to 180&deg;.
~B. They add to 90&deg;#Complementary angles add to 90&deg;.
~C. They are always congruent#Supplementary angles are not always congruent.
~D. They are always vertical angles#Supplementary angles do not have to be vertical.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q24::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.1<br><br>Question:<br>If two angles form a linear pair and one angle measures 70&deg;, what is the measure of the other angle? {
~A. 70&deg;#Linear-pair angles are not always congruent.
=B. 110&deg;#Correct. 180&deg; - 70&deg; = 110&deg;.
~C. 120&deg;#This does not add to 180&deg; with 70&deg;.
~D. 20&deg;#This does not add to 180&deg; with 70&deg;.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q25::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which reason supports m&angle;A + m&angle;B = 180&deg; when &angle;A and &angle;B are supplementary? {
~A. Vertical angles are congruent#This does not explain supplementary angle measures.
~B. Definition of midpoint#Midpoint applies to segments.
~C. The angles look adjacent#Appearance is not the definition.
=D. Definition of supplementary angles#Correct. Supplementary angles add to 180&deg; by definition.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q26::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q26<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.1<br><br>Question:<br>A proof states two lines are parallel because corresponding angles are congruent. What type of evidence is being used? {
~A. Appearance-based evidence#The reason is a line-angle relationship.
=B. A line and angle relationship#Correct. Corresponding angle congruence can support parallel-line reasoning.
~C. A measurement guess#The statement is not based on guessing.
~D. A counterexample#The statement supports a conclusion, not disproves one.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q27::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q27<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.5<br><br>Question:<br>What is the purpose of reasons in a proof? {
~A. To make the proof longer#Reasons justify, not lengthen, a proof.
~B. To replace all given information#Given information remains part of the proof.
=C. To justify why each statement follows#Correct. Reasons connect statements to definitions, properties, or theorems.
~D. To avoid mathematical vocabulary#Proof reasons require precise vocabulary.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q28::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q28<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.5<br><br>Question:<br>Which pair best describes a basic proof setup? {
=A. Given and prove#Correct. A proof often begins with given information and a statement to prove.
~B. Guess and drawing#Proofs require reasoning, not guessing.
~C. Opinion and example#Proof is not based on opinion.
~D. Color and shape#Visual features alone do not define a proof setup.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q29::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q29<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.5<br><br>Question:<br>What is the first step in a proof by contradiction? {
~A. State the conclusion without support#Proof requires support.
=B. Assume the opposite of what you want to prove#Correct. Contradiction proof begins with the negation of the desired conclusion.
~C. Find one example that looks correct#Contradiction proof does not begin with one visual example.
~D. Erase the original claim#The original claim remains the goal.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q30::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q30<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.5<br><br>Question:<br>In a proof by contradiction, what does an impossible result show? {
~A. The original statement must be rejected#The impossible result rejects the opposite assumption.
~B. The proof has become a counterexample#Contradiction proof is not the same as a counterexample.
=C. The opposite assumption cannot be true#Correct. This supports the original statement.
~D. The diagram should be ignored#The reasoning, not ignoring diagrams, drives the conclusion.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q31::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q31<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.5<br><br>Question:<br>Which proof step is incomplete? {
=A. Statement: &angle;1 &cong; &angle;2. Reason: It looks true.#Correct. Appearance is not a valid proof reason.
~B. Statement: &angle;1 and &angle;2 are vertical angles. Reason: Given.#This statement has a valid reason.
~C. Statement: &angle;1 &cong; &angle;2. Reason: Vertical angles are congruent.#This statement has a valid reason.
~D. Statement: m&angle;A + m&angle;B = 180&deg;. Reason: Definition of supplementary angles.#This statement has a valid reason.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q32::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q32<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.5<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement best describes a valid geometric proof? {
~A. A drawing that appears correct#Appearance alone is not proof.
~B. A list of conclusions with no reasons#A proof must justify conclusions.
~C. A single example that matches the claim#A single example does not prove a universal statement.
=D. A logical argument with statements supported by reasons#Correct. Valid proofs connect each statement to a reason.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q33::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q33<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.6<br><br>Question:<br>What is a counterexample? {
~A. A second example that supports a claim#A counterexample disproves rather than supports.
~B. A drawing that looks accurate#Appearance is not the defining feature.
=C. One example that shows a statement is false#Correct. One valid counterexample disproves a universal claim.
~D. A proof with no reasons#A proof needs reasons.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q34::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q34<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which counterexample disproves the claim All rectangles are squares? {
=A. A rectangle with side lengths 3 and 8#Correct. It is a rectangle but not a square.
~B. A square with side length 6#A square does not disprove the claim.
~C. A triangle with side lengths 3, 4, and 5#A triangle is not a rectangle.
~D. A circle with diameter 8#A circle is not a rectangle.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q35::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q35<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which counterexample disproves the claim All acute angles are congruent? {
~A. Two 45&deg; angles#These are congruent and do not disprove the claim.
=B. A 30&deg; angle and a 50&deg; angle#Correct. Both are acute, but they are not congruent.
~C. A 90&deg; angle and a 45&deg; angle#A 90&deg; angle is not acute.
~D. Two right angles#Right angles are not acute.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q36::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q36<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.6<br><br>Question:<br>Why is the argument The segments are congruent because they look equal invalid? {
~A. Congruent segments cannot appear in diagrams#Congruent segments can appear in diagrams.
~B. Segments never need evidence#Segment congruence requires evidence.
~C. The conclusion is always false#The conclusion may be true, but the reason is invalid.
=D. It uses appearance instead of a valid reason#Correct. A valid argument needs evidence such as markings, measurements, or a proven relationship.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q37::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q37<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.6<br><br>Question:<br>Why is one example not enough to prove a universal claim? {
=A. A universal claim must work for every case#Correct. Universal claims require reasoning that covers all cases.
~B. One example can only disprove statements#One example can support exploration, but not prove every case.
~C. Universal claims are never true#Some universal claims are true when proven.
~D. Counterexamples only work with numbers#Counterexamples can use geometric figures.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q38::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q38<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which argument is valid? {
~A. The lines are parallel because they look parallel#Appearance alone is not valid evidence.
~B. The angle is right because it seems close to 90&deg;#Approximate appearance is not proof.
=C. The angles are congruent because they are vertical angles#Correct. The conclusion is supported by a known angle relationship.
~D. The segments are equal because they are drawn darkly#Line darkness is not mathematical evidence.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q39::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q39<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.5<br><br>Question:<br>Which explanation best supports the conclusion &angle;1 &cong; &angle;2? {
~A. They appear to have the same measure#Appearance alone is not proof.
~B. They are near each other in the diagram#Location alone does not prove congruence.
~C. They are both drawn with dark lines#Line darkness is not mathematical evidence.
=D. They are vertical angles, and vertical angles are congruent#Correct. This gives evidence and a valid reason.
}

::GEO_U01_Assessment_Q40::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_A_Q40<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.1<br><br>Question:<br>If two angles form a linear pair and one angle measures 65&deg;, what is the measure of the other angle? {
~A. 65&deg;#Linear-pair angles are not always congruent.
=B. 115&deg;#Correct. 180&deg; - 65&deg; = 115&deg;.
~C. 125&deg;#This does not make a sum of 180&deg; with 65&deg;.
~D. 25&deg;#This does not make a sum of 180&deg; with 65&deg;.
}

